data.content:
## .NET 8 Migration: `TimedExecutor`

### Key Breaking Changes

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| `ThreadAbortException` | Never raised; catch is dead code |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.Wait throws AggregateException on fault or cancellation
            task.Wait(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }

        // Unwrap single inner exception; preserves original stack trace
        return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
}
```

### If the work itself needs to observe cancellation

Pass `CancellationToken` into the delegate so it can cooperatively exit:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

### Pitfalls

1. **No forced termination** — `Thread.Abort()` killed work mid-flight; `CancellationToken` is *cooperative*. If `work` ignores the token (e.g., blocks on unmanaged I/O), the task keeps running after timeout. There is no .NET 8 equivalent of forced abort.

2. **Exception semantics changed** — The old code wrapped errors in `AggregateException`. Using `GetAwaiter().GetResult()` unwraps to the original exception. If callers catch `AggregateException`, update them.

3. **`Task.Run` uses the ThreadPool** — The original code created a dedicated `Thread`. For long-running work, use `Task.Factory.StartNew(..., TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning)` instead of `Task.Run`.

4. **`default(T)` race condition was already a bug** — The original code had a subtle issue: `result` was a captured local read after `thread.Join`, with no memory barrier guaranteeing visibility. `Task<T>` eliminates this entirely.

5. **Deadlock risk** — Calling `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` on a UI/single-threaded `SynchronizationContext` can deadlock. Fine in console/server apps; in UI apps use `await` instead.
